             QUESTION 1
Deadlock condition:
    P1 locks R1 and waits for R2.
    P2 locks R2 and waits for R1.
    Both wait for each other, so a deadlock occurs.
Optimization:
Make both processes lock resources in the same order (e.g., R1 first, then R2).
This prevents deadlock.
  
            QUESTION 2
Cause:
     Processes are frequently waiting for I/O (disk, keyboard, network, etc.).
     So, the CPU remains idle even though processes are ready.
System-level optimization:
        Reduce I/O waiting time using faster I/O devices or I/O buffering.
        This keeps the CPU busy and improves CPU utilization.
  
            QUESTION 3
Memory issue:
        The program has 25 threads, so many memory pages are used.
        This causes frequent page faults, which slows down performance.
Two optimizations:
         Reduce the number of threads to use less memory.
         Optimize heap memory usage by freeing unused memory and avoiding unnecessary allocations.
  
               QUESTION 4
  Root cause:
           P5 is in the lowest priority queue (Q3).
            Higher-priority queues (Q1 and Q2) keep getting CPU time.
            So, P5 never gets a chance to execute, causing starvation.
Optimization:
        Use Aging (increase the priority of processes waiting for a long time).
        This ensures P5 eventually gets CPU time and improves fairness.
  
                 QUESTION 5
  Reason:
             The time quantum is very small (1 ms), so the CPU switches processes very often.
             High I/O wait also causes frequent context switches.
             This creates high overhead and reduces performance.
   Two optimizations:
        Increase the time quantum (e.g., from 1 ms to 5 ms) to reduce context switches.
        Reduce I/O waiting using faster I/O devices or I/O buffering to improve CPU efficiency.
